INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Proprietary fund cash flow statement direct method requirement
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Thorndale''s water enterprise fund controller is preparing the annual statement of cash flows for fiscal year 2026. Total operating cash receipts are $9,400,000, and operating cash disbursements are $7,200,000. The controller proposes using the indirect method, starting with operating income. Under GASB 34, which cash flow presentation method is required for proprietary fund statements?', '["The direct method is required, showing major classes of operating receipts and disbursements", "The indirect method is required, reconciling operating income to net cash from operations", "Either the direct or indirect method may be used at the government''s discretion", "The direct method is required only for enterprise funds, while internal service funds may use the indirect method"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34, proprietary fund statements of cash flows must use the direct method, presenting major classes of operating cash receipts and disbursements (GASB 34, par. 105). This differs from FASB standards, which permit either method. The indirect method reconciliation is still required as a supplemental schedule, but it cannot serve as the primary presentation. The controller''s proposed indirect-method-only approach does not comply with GASB requirements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Government-wide indirect expense allocation decisions
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Bridgewater''s finance director is preparing the government-wide statement of activities. General government administrative costs total $4,800,000, which support all functional departments. The director debates whether to allocate these costs across functions or report them as a separate line item. Under GASB 34, evaluate the most appropriate treatment of indirect administrative expenses on the statement of activities.', '["Allocate all $4,800,000 proportionally across functions because GASB 34 mandates full cost allocation to achieve accurate net expense by function", "Report indirect costs as a general revenue offset rather than a functional expense to avoid distorting program cost comparisons", "Allocate only the portion directly traceable to specific functions and report the remainder as an extraordinary item", "Report general government as a separate functional line without allocation, because GASB 34 does not require indirect cost allocation and the direct presentation avoids arbitrary methodologies"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 42), governments may report indirect expenses as a separate line or allocate them to functional categories, but allocation is not required. When a government chooses not to allocate, general government administration appears as its own functional line. Mandatory allocation (choice A) is incorrect because GASB 34 permits but does not require it. Reporting as a general revenue offset (choice B) misclassifies expenses, and extraordinary items were eliminated by GASB 56.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Effect of interfund reimbursement vs transfer on government-wide statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Northfield''s general fund pays $175,000 for equipment maintenance that should have been charged to the parks special revenue fund. The parks fund reimburses the general fund $175,000 before year-end. In preparing the government-wide statement of activities, how does classifying this as a reimbursement rather than a transfer affect total governmental activities expenses?', '["Total governmental activities expenses remain unchanged because both funds are governmental activities and the reimbursement is eliminated", "Total governmental activities expenses decrease by $175,000 because the reimbursement offsets the original expenditure", "Total governmental activities expenses increase by $175,000 because both the expenditure and the reimbursement are reported", "Total governmental activities expenses decrease by $350,000 because both the expenditure and the reimbursement are eliminated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 41), interfund reimbursements are not reported as transfers or revenues. The paying fund reduces its expenditures and the receiving fund records the expenditure, so the net effect is a reclassification within governmental activities. In government-wide consolidation, expenses appear in the parks function rather than general government. Total governmental activities expenses remain unchanged because the reimbursement merely redirects which function bears the cost. Choice B incorrectly suggests a net expense reduction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Effect of GASB 68 blended discount rate on net pension liability
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Lakewood''s single-employer pension plan has a projected asset depletion date of year 22. Benefits payable in years 1 through 22 are discounted at the expected long-term rate of return on plan assets of 7.0%. Benefits payable after year 22 are discounted at a 20-year AA-rated municipal bond index rate of 3.5%. What is the most likely effect of the blended discount rate compared to using a single 7.0% rate for all periods?', '["The total pension liability decreases, resulting in a smaller net pension liability", "The total pension liability increases, resulting in a larger net pension liability", "The total pension liability remains unchanged because the blended rate only affects pension expense timing", "The net pension liability decreases because the lower rate reduces projected benefit payments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 68 (par. 26-28), the discount rate is a blended single rate combining the expected return on plan assets for the funded horizon and a municipal bond index rate for the unfunded horizon. Using 3.5% instead of 7.0% for post-depletion benefits increases the present value of those benefits, raising the total pension liability and therefore the net pension liability. Choice A incorrectly reverses the relationship between discount rates and present values. A lower discount rate always increases the present value of future obligations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Evaluating whether ISF should be reclassified as enterprise fund
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ridgecrest operates an internal service fund for fleet management that serves both city departments (80% of billings) and three neighboring towns under intergovernmental agreements (20% of billings). The fund''s external billings have grown from 5% to 20% over three years. The city auditor questions whether the fund should be reclassified as an enterprise fund. Evaluate the most appropriate classification under GASB 34.', '["The fund should remain an internal service fund because a majority of billings serve internal departments, but the external activity must be disclosed", "The fund must be reclassified as an enterprise fund because any external billing activity triggers the mandatory enterprise fund criteria under GASB 34", "The fund should be reclassified as an enterprise fund because the 20% external billing exceeds a de minimis threshold and the trend suggests the fund''s primary purpose is shifting", "The fund should remain an internal service fund because reclassification requires the governing body to pass a formal resolution changing the fund designation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 67-68), an enterprise fund is required when fees are charged to external users as the primary revenue source or when legally required. An internal service fund predominantly serves internal departments. Since 80% of Ridgecrest''s fleet billings remain internal, the fund''s predominant purpose is still internal service delivery. Choice B is incorrect because GASB 34 does not mandate enterprise fund classification merely because some external billing exists. However, the city should disclose the external component and monitor whether the balance shifts.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Effect of consumption vs purchases method on fund balance
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Greenbriar''s general fund purchases $420,000 of office supplies on July 1, 2026. By December 31, $290,000 worth has been consumed, leaving $130,000 in inventory. The town uses the purchases method for supply accounting. How does the choice of the purchases method rather than the consumption method affect year-end fund balance and the balance sheet?', '["Fund balance is $130,000 higher under the purchases method because unconsumed supplies are reported as assets", "Fund balance is the same under both methods because GASB 54 requires a nonspendable reserve for inventory regardless of method", "Fund balance is $130,000 lower under the purchases method because the full $420,000 is reported as expenditures, with $130,000 offset by a nonspendable fund balance reserve", "Fund balance is $420,000 lower under the purchases method because no inventory asset is recorded on the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB standards, the purchases method records the full $420,000 as expenditures when purchased. If ending inventory is significant, the balance sheet reports a $130,000 inventory asset with a corresponding $130,000 nonspendable fund balance reserve, leaving spendable fund balance $130,000 lower than under the consumption method. Under the consumption method, only $290,000 would be expensed. Choice D is incorrect because the purchases method still reports significant inventory on the balance sheet with a nonspendable offset under GASB 54.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Effect of pension contribution after measurement date
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Millbrook County''s fiscal year ends December 31, and its pension plan uses a measurement date of September 30. The county''s annual pension contribution of $1,850,000 is paid on November 15, 2026, falling after the September 30 measurement date but before the December 31 reporting date. Under GASB 68, what is the most likely effect of this timing difference on the government-wide financial statements?', '["The $1,850,000 contribution reduces the net pension liability immediately because it was paid before the reporting date", "The $1,850,000 contribution is reported as a deferred outflow of resources and recognized as a reduction of the net pension liability in the following measurement period", "The $1,850,000 contribution is reported as a prepaid asset on the statement of net position", "The $1,850,000 contribution is expensed in full as pension expense for the current reporting period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 68 (par. 57), employer contributions made after the measurement date but before the reporting date are reported as deferred outflows of resources on the employer''s statement of net position. These amounts are recognized as a reduction of net pension liability in the next measurement period. Choice A incorrectly assumes the contribution reduces the current period''s net pension liability, but the liability is measured as of September 30, before the payment. Choice C misclassifies the item as a prepaid asset rather than a deferred outflow, which is a distinct financial statement element under GASB 63.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Identifying program vs general revenues on statement of activities
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Clearfield reports the following revenues for fiscal year 2026: property taxes of $32,000,000, a federal public safety grant of $2,800,000, municipal court fines of $640,000, building permit fees of $1,350,000, unrestricted state revenue sharing of $4,100,000, and investment income of $560,000. Under GASB 34, what is the total amount of program revenues?', '["$37,350,000", "$41,450,000", "$4,790,000", "$8,890,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 48-52), program revenues include charges for services (building permits $1,350,000, court fines $640,000) and program-specific grants (federal public safety grant $2,800,000), totaling $4,790,000. Property taxes, unrestricted state revenue sharing, and investment income are general revenues because they support the government as a whole rather than specific programs. Choice D incorrectly includes unrestricted state revenue sharing as a program revenue, but unrestricted intergovernmental revenue is a general revenue under GASB 34.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Calculating restricted net position on government-wide statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Glendale reports the following on its government-wide statement of net position: a state highway grant with $2,800,000 unspent, $1,400,000 in debt service reserves required by bond covenants, $3,200,000 committed by city council for parks expansion, and $950,000 assigned by the finance director for vehicle replacement. Under GASB 34, what amount qualifies as restricted net position?', '["$5,150,000", "$4,200,000", "$8,350,000", "$2,800,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 34-36), restricted net position includes resources constrained by external parties (creditors, grantors, other governments) or by law. The state highway grant ($2,800,000) is restricted by the grantor, and debt service reserves ($1,400,000) are restricted by bond covenants, totaling $4,200,000. Committed ($3,200,000) and assigned ($950,000) amounts reflect internal constraints and are classified as unrestricted net position on the government-wide statements. Choice A incorrectly adds committed resources to the restricted category.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — GASB 34 reconciliation requirement
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, what is the purpose of the reconciliation presented between governmental fund financial statements and government-wide financial statements?', '["To explain differences caused by the different measurement focus and basis of accounting used in each set of statements", "To eliminate interfund transactions between governmental and proprietary funds", "To convert proprietary fund statements from accrual to modified accrual basis", "To adjust fiduciary fund balances for inclusion in the government-wide statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 77), a reconciliation is required to explain the differences between the governmental fund balance sheet and the government-wide statement of net position, and between the governmental fund statement of revenues, expenditures, and changes in fund balances and the government-wide statement of activities. These differences arise from different measurement focuses and bases of accounting. Choice D is incorrect because fiduciary funds are excluded from government-wide statements entirely.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Proprietary fund operating statement title
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, what is the required title of the operating statement for proprietary funds?', '["Statement of revenues, expenditures, and changes in fund balances", "Statement of revenues, expenses, and changes in fund net position", "Statement of activities", "Income statement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34, proprietary funds present a statement of revenues, expenses, and changes in fund net position (sometimes referenced as changes in net position). This title reflects the full accrual, economic resources focus of proprietary funds. Choice A describes the governmental fund operating statement, which uses the term expenditures rather than expenses. Choice C is the government-wide operating statement. Choice D applies to commercial entities, not governmental funds.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Effect of GASB 87 on proprietary fund operating income classification
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Ashbury''s water enterprise fund enters a 10-year lease for treatment plant equipment on January 1, 2026, with annual payments of $280,000. Under GASB 87, the fund records amortization of the right-of-use asset and interest on the lease liability. The controller asks how the lease affects the distinction between operating and nonoperating items. What is the most likely effect on the enterprise fund''s operating income?', '["Both amortization and interest are reported as operating expenses because the leased asset supports operations", "Amortization of the right-of-use asset is an operating expense, while interest on the lease liability is a nonoperating expense, splitting the lease cost across two categories", "The full annual lease payment of $280,000 is reported as an operating expense with no separate interest component", "Both amortization and interest are reported as nonoperating expenses because GASB 87 leases are financing transactions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 87 and GASB 34 (par. 100-102), proprietary fund operating expenses include costs of providing services, while nonoperating items include interest expense. Amortization of the right-of-use asset is an operating expense because it represents the cost of using the asset in operations. Interest on the lease liability is classified as nonoperating expense, consistent with other interest costs. Choice A incorrectly classifies interest as operating, and choice C treats the lease as a rental expense rather than applying GASB 87''s asset-liability model.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Encumbrance closing entries at year-end (lapsing vs non-lapsing)
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The Town of Woodridge has $245,000 in outstanding encumbrances at the December 31, 2026, fiscal year-end for equipment ordered but not yet received. The town''s policy treats encumbrances as lapsing at year-end. Under GASB standards, what journal entry should Woodridge record in the general fund at year-end?', '["Debit expenditures $245,000 and credit accounts payable $245,000 to accrue the liability", "Debit encumbrances $245,000 and credit fund balance—committed $245,000 to reclassify the reserve", "No entry is required because encumbrances automatically carry forward to the next fiscal year", "Debit budgetary fund balance—reserve for encumbrances $245,000 and credit encumbrances $245,000 to reverse the outstanding commitments"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB standards, when encumbrances lapse at year-end, the outstanding encumbrance entry is reversed: debit budgetary fund balance—reserve for encumbrances and credit encumbrances for $245,000. The goods have not been received, so no expenditure or liability is recorded. In the subsequent year, the purchase order must be re-encumbered against the new year''s appropriations. Choice A incorrectly accrues a liability for undelivered goods. Choice C describes non-lapsing treatment, which would keep encumbrances open.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — GASB 34 four categories of cash flows for proprietary funds
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, the proprietary fund statement of cash flows classifies activities into how many categories?', '["Four: operating, noncapital financing, capital and related financing, and investing", "Three: operating, investing, and financing", "Five: operating, noncapital financing, capital financing, investing, and fiduciary", "Two: operating and nonoperating"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 105-114), proprietary fund cash flows are classified into four categories: operating activities, noncapital financing activities, capital and related financing activities, and investing activities. This differs from FASB''s three-category model (operating, investing, financing). Choice B describes the FASB classification used by commercial entities. The four-category model separates financing into capital-related and noncapital activities for greater transparency.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Evaluating whether government should adopt modified approach for new infrastructure
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Marsfield recently completed a $45 million water distribution system. The county engineer proposes using the modified approach under GASB 34, citing cost savings from avoiding depreciation calculations. The system requires condition assessments every three years at $180,000 each. Annual depreciation under the standard approach would be $900,000 over 50 years. Annual preservation spending averages $650,000. Evaluate whether the modified approach is appropriate.', '["The modified approach is not appropriate because it applies only to infrastructure assets of governmental activities reported in the government-wide statements, not to proprietary fund assets like water systems", "The modified approach is appropriate because the county can establish a condition target and perform regular assessments, saving $720,000 annually by avoiding depreciation", "The modified approach is appropriate for any infrastructure asset regardless of fund type, provided condition assessments are completed every three years", "The modified approach is not appropriate because annual preservation costs exceed the three-year amortized assessment cost, making depreciation more cost-effective"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 23-25), the modified approach is available only for eligible infrastructure assets of governmental activities reported in government-wide statements. A water distribution system accounted for in an enterprise fund (proprietary) is not eligible for the modified approach; it must be depreciated. Choice B incorrectly applies the modified approach to a proprietary fund asset. Choice C is wrong because fund type matters—only governmental activity infrastructure qualifies. The cost savings argument is irrelevant to the eligibility determination.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Imposed nonexchange revenue recognition timing for fines
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Kendrick''s code enforcement division assesses $560,000 in environmental code violation fines during fiscal year 2026. By December 31, $410,000 has been collected. An additional $80,000 is expected within the 60-day availability period, and $70,000 is considered uncollectible. Under GASB 33 and modified accrual, how much fine revenue should the general fund recognize?', '["$560,000", "$410,000", "$490,000", "$480,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 33, fines are classified as imposed nonexchange revenues. Under modified accrual, revenue is recognized when measurable and available. The city should recognize the $410,000 already collected plus $80,000 expected within 60 days, totaling $490,000. The $70,000 deemed uncollectible is excluded. Choice A incorrectly includes the full levy without considering collectibility or availability. Choice D ($480,000) appears to subtract only the uncollectible amount without applying the availability criterion. Choice B omits the $80,000 expected within the availability period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Effect of GASB 34 major fund reporting on nonmajor aggregation
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Stonebridge has eight special revenue funds. Under the GASB 34 quantitative criteria, two funds qualify as major and must be reported individually. The finance director asks how the remaining six nonmajor special revenue funds should be presented in the fund financial statements. What is the most likely presentation requirement?', '["Each nonmajor fund must be presented in a separate column regardless of major fund status", "The six nonmajor funds are aggregated into a single ''other governmental funds'' column on the fund financial statements, with individual detail available in combining statements", "Nonmajor funds are excluded from the fund financial statements and reported only in the government-wide statements", "The six nonmajor funds are combined with the general fund column for presentation purposes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 75-76), nonmajor governmental funds are aggregated into a single column labeled other governmental funds on the governmental fund financial statements. Individual fund detail is presented in combining statements, which are supplementary information. Choice A is incorrect because only major funds require separate columnar presentation. Choice C is wrong because nonmajor funds are still reported in fund financial statements, just in aggregate. Choice D incorrectly merges them with the general fund.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — GASB 63 net position terminology
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 63, what term replaced ''net assets'' in government-wide financial statements?', '["Fund balance", "Equity", "Retained earnings", "Net position"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 63 (Financial Reporting of Deferred Outflows of Resources, Deferred Inflows of Resources, and Net Position), the residual measure in government-wide and proprietary fund statements was renamed from net assets to net position. This change accommodated the introduction of deferred outflows and deferred inflows as distinct financial statement elements. Choice A (fund balance) applies to governmental fund statements, not government-wide statements. Choice C (retained earnings) is a commercial accounting term not used in governmental reporting.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — GASB 34 noncapital financing activities in proprietary fund cash flows
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, which transaction is classified as a noncapital financing activity on the proprietary fund statement of cash flows?', '["Payment of bond interest on revenue bonds used to construct a treatment facility", "Operating grant received from the state for water quality testing", "Cash received from customer water service charges", "Cash received from the general fund as a subsidy transfer"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 109-110), noncapital financing activities include borrowings and transfers not related to capital asset acquisition. A subsidy transfer from the general fund is a noncapital financing activity because it represents a nonoperating cash inflow unrelated to capital purposes. Choice A (bond interest for capital construction) belongs in capital and related financing activities. Choice B (operating grant) is classified as operating or noncapital depending on context. Choice C (customer charges) is an operating activity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Interfund services used and provided definition
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, what are interfund services used and provided?', '["One-way transfers of resources from one fund to another without repayment", "Loans between funds that must be repaid within one year", "Reciprocal transactions between funds for goods or services at approximate external exchange value", "Reimbursements for expenditures initially recorded in the wrong fund"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 407-408), interfund services used and provided are reciprocal interfund activities in which one fund provides goods or services to another fund at prices approximating their external exchange value. Examples include internal service fund charges to other departments. Choice A describes interfund transfers, which are nonreciprocal. Choice B describes interfund loans. Choice D describes interfund reimbursements, which correct initial recording errors.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Applying GRaSPP mnemonic to identify governmental fund types
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Hawthorne receives a $1,200,000 endowment from a deceased resident. The will specifies that the principal must be permanently invested and only the investment income may fund public library programs. Under GASB standards, which governmental fund type should the city use?', '["Special revenue fund", "Private-purpose trust fund", "General fund", "Permanent fund"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34, a permanent fund accounts for resources legally restricted so that only earnings—not principal—may be used for the government''s own programs. Because the endowment benefits the government''s library programs (not external parties), it belongs in a permanent fund. Choice A is incorrect because a special revenue fund accounts for restricted revenue streams, not endowment principal. Choice B would apply if the beneficiaries were external parties rather than government programs.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — GASB 34 requirement for infrastructure capitalization
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, which type of asset must be reported on the government-wide statement of net position but is excluded from governmental fund balance sheets?', '["Long-term receivables from other governments", "Prepaid insurance and supply inventories", "Capital assets such as roads, buildings, and equipment", "Cash and investments held in trust"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 18-22), capital assets including infrastructure (roads, bridges, water systems), buildings, and equipment are reported on the government-wide statement of net position using the economic resources measurement focus. Governmental fund balance sheets use the current financial resources focus and do not report long-term assets. Choice A is partially correct but less fundamental than capital assets. Choice B (prepaid items and inventories) appears on both governmental fund and government-wide statements.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Computing change in fund balance for debt service fund
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Winslow''s debt service fund reports the following for fiscal year 2026: property tax revenue of $4,200,000, investment income of $85,000, bond principal payments of $2,500,000, bond interest payments of $1,600,000, and a transfer in from the general fund of $350,000. What is the change in fund balance for the debt service fund?', '["$100,000 increase", "$535,000 increase", "$185,000 increase", "$4,285,000 increase"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under modified accrual accounting, the debt service fund''s change in fund balance equals revenues plus other financing sources minus expenditures minus other financing uses. Revenues ($4,200,000 + $85,000 = $4,285,000) plus transfer in ($350,000) minus expenditures ($2,500,000 + $1,600,000 = $4,100,000) = $535,000 increase. Choice A ($100,000) incorrectly omits the transfer in. Choice C ($185,000) incorrectly omits the transfer in and miscalculates. Choice D counts only revenues without netting expenditures.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Classifying bond issuance costs in governmental vs proprietary funds
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Davenport issues $20,000,000 in general obligation bonds through its capital projects fund, paying $180,000 in underwriter fees at closing. In the same year, the city''s water enterprise fund issues $8,000,000 in revenue bonds, paying $95,000 in issuance costs. Under GASB 65, how should bond issuance costs be reported in each fund type?', '["The $180,000 is an expenditure in the capital projects fund, while the $95,000 is amortized over the bond term in the enterprise fund", "Both amounts are capitalized as deferred charges and amortized over the bond terms", "Both the $180,000 and $95,000 are recognized as expenses or expenditures in the period incurred", "The $180,000 is netted against bond proceeds in the capital projects fund, while the $95,000 is expensed in the enterprise fund"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 65 (par. 12), bond issuance costs, except prepaid insurance, are recognized as an expense (proprietary funds) or expenditure (governmental funds) in the period incurred. Neither amount is deferred or amortized. The $180,000 is an expenditure of the capital projects fund, and the $95,000 is an expense of the enterprise fund, both in the current period. Choice A incorrectly suggests amortization for proprietary funds. Choice B describes the pre-GASB 65 treatment that was eliminated.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Effect of GASB 34 special item on statement of activities placement
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Eastport sells a community recreation center for $6,200,000, producing a $1,800,000 gain. The city council approved the sale as part of a strategic asset reduction initiative, and the sale was a one-time event within management''s control. The finance director considers reporting the gain as a general revenue on the government-wide statement of activities. What is the most likely effect of properly classifying this transaction under GASB 34?', '["The $1,800,000 gain is reported as a program revenue under the general government function", "The $1,800,000 gain is reported as a special item, presented separately after general revenues and before the change in net position", "The $1,800,000 gain is reported as a general revenue because asset disposals are not program-specific", "The $1,800,000 gain is reported as an extraordinary item below the change in net position line"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 55-56), a special item is a transaction within the control of management that is either unusual or infrequent. The council-approved, one-time sale qualifies as a special item and must be reported separately on the statement of activities, after general revenues but before the change in net position. Choice C incorrectly classifies it as a general revenue. Choice D references extraordinary items, which GASB 56 eliminated from governmental accounting. Special items receive prominent separate disclosure.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Evaluating whether a joint venture requires equity-method reporting
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The cities of Clearmont and Ridgedale each contribute $5,000,000 to create the Regional Solid Waste Authority. Each city appoints three of six board members. The Authority operates a landfill, sets its own user fees, and issues debt without city guarantees. Each city has a 50% ongoing financial interest in the Authority''s net position, which totals $12,400,000. Under GASB 14, evaluate the most appropriate reporting treatment for Clearmont.', '["Report the investment using the equity method at $6,200,000 on Clearmont''s government-wide statement of net position, with Clearmont''s share of changes in net position recognized annually", "Report the Authority as a blended component unit because Clearmont appoints half the board members", "Report only the initial $5,000,000 contribution as an asset with no ongoing adjustments", "Report the Authority as a discretely presented component unit because both cities share governance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 14 (par. 69-73) and GASB 61, a jointly governed organization in which a government has an ongoing financial interest is reported using the equity method. Clearmont has a 50% equity interest and should report $6,200,000 (50% × $12,400,000) on its statement of net position, adjusting annually for its share of changes. Choice B is incorrect because the Authority is not a component unit of either city alone—neither city appoints a voting majority. Choice C ignores the ongoing financial interest requirement for equity-method reporting.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: hard / L3 — Effect of GASB 65 on deferred charges reclassification
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The County of Brenton previously reported $2,300,000 in debt issuance costs as a deferred charge on its government-wide statement of net position, amortizing them over 20-year bond terms. The county implements GASB 65 for the first time. What is the most likely effect of GASB 65 implementation on the county''s government-wide financial statements?', '["Net position increases because GASB 65 reclassifies the deferred charge as a deferred outflow of resources", "Net position is unaffected because the deferred charge is transferred from assets to a new deferred outflow line item", "Net position decreases because the $2,300,000 deferred charge is removed from the statement of net position and recognized as a cumulative adjustment to beginning net position", "Net position decreases by only the current year''s amortization amount because GASB 65 is applied prospectively"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under GASB 65 (par. 12 and 17), debt issuance costs (except prepaid insurance) are no longer eligible to be reported as deferred charges. Upon implementation, previously capitalized issuance costs are removed from the statement of net position, decreasing beginning net position through a cumulative-effect adjustment. Choice A is incorrect because issuance costs do not meet the definition of a deferred outflow under GASB 63. Choice D is wrong because GASB 65 requires retroactive application with restatement of beginning balances, not prospective-only treatment.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Evaluating fund balance spending policy with mixed restricted/committed/assigned
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Eastgate''s general fund has $3,400,000 restricted by a state grant for road repairs, $2,200,000 committed by council resolution for road repairs, and $800,000 assigned by the city manager for road repairs. The city has adopted a formal spending policy under GASB 54 that specifies committed resources are spent before restricted resources for road repair purposes. A $4,500,000 road repair project is approved. Evaluate which fund balance classifications are reduced.', '["$3,400,000 restricted is spent first, then $1,100,000 of committed is spent, for a total of $4,500,000", "$2,200,000 committed is spent first, then $800,000 assigned, then $1,500,000 of restricted, for a total of $4,500,000", "$800,000 assigned is spent first, then $2,200,000 committed, then $1,500,000 of restricted, for a total of $4,500,000", "$2,200,000 committed is spent first, then $2,300,000 of restricted is spent, for a total of $4,500,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 54 (par. 18), the default spending order is restricted first, then committed, then assigned, then unassigned. However, GASB 54 permits governments to adopt a formal spending policy that changes this order. Eastgate''s policy specifies committed before restricted. Therefore, $2,200,000 committed is spent first, then $2,300,000 of restricted, totaling $4,500,000. Assigned resources ($800,000) are not consumed. Choice A follows the default order, ignoring the city''s adopted spending policy. Choice B incorrectly inserts assigned between committed and restricted.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — GASB 34 component of basic financial statements
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'Under GASB 34, which of the following is a required component of a government''s basic financial statements?', '["Notes to the financial statements", "Statistical section tables", "Transmittal letter from the chief executive", "Independent auditor''s report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 6-8), basic financial statements include government-wide financial statements, fund financial statements, and notes to the financial statements. Notes are an integral part of the basic financial statements. The statistical section, transmittal letter, and auditor''s report are components of the broader comprehensive annual financial report but are not part of the basic financial statements themselves. Choice B (statistical section) is supplementary information, not a basic financial statement component.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Reporting capital contributions vs operating grants in proprietary funds
(2, 'Governmental Accounting', 'The City of Brookdale''s wastewater enterprise fund receives a $2,600,000 federal grant to construct a new pump station and a $420,000 state grant for annual water quality testing operations. Under GASB 34, how should the enterprise fund classify these grants on the statement of revenues, expenses, and changes in fund net position?', '["Both grants are reported as operating revenues because they support fund operations", "The $2,600,000 is an operating revenue and the $420,000 is a capital contribution", "Both grants are reported as capital contributions below the nonoperating section", "The $2,600,000 is a capital contribution reported below nonoperating items, and the $420,000 is an operating grant reported as nonoperating revenue"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under GASB 34 (par. 100-102), proprietary fund statements distinguish operating revenues, nonoperating revenues, and capital contributions. The $2,600,000 construction grant is a capital contribution reported after nonoperating items because it funds capital asset acquisition. The $420,000 operating grant supports ongoing testing activities and is reported as nonoperating revenue (grants are generally nonoperating even when they support operations). Choice A incorrectly classifies grants as operating revenues. Choice C misclassifies the operating grant as a capital contribution.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
